+++### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs: AI Providers and Token Budgets
+++
+++- **provider_kind:** Identifies the foundational AI provider protocol engine. Supported choices are `gemini` (Google Gemini 2.5 Flash/Pro with native function calling and multimodal spec support), `openai` (OpenAI GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini, and compatible gateways implementing standard JSON schema tool definitions), and `ollama` (local air-gapped runtimes for private edge models like Llama 3 and DeepSeek-R1). Selecting a kind binds the request serializer, streaming parser, and error mapper.
+++- **display_name:** The descriptive title assigned to the provider instance (for example, "Google Gemini Workspace" or "Local Ollama Runtime"). Scoped to the workspace or server tier, this label identifies the provider across cards in `/admin/ai`, the failover priority stack, and the audit ledger.
+++- **base_url:** The root network URL for provider API communications. Cloud providers require secure HTTPS endpoints (`https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com` or `https://api.openai.com/v1`), whereas local Ollama deployments typically connect over internal loopback (`http://127.0.0.1:11434`). Outbound calls are subject to network policy and workspace air-gap restrictions.
+++- **api_key:** The secret API key used to authenticate upstream cloud requests. Submitted over TLS, stored encrypted at rest via PostgreSQL envelope encryption, and never echoed back to client browsers. In `AiProviderEditorDrawer`, input fields adhere to strict security invariants by using standard masked inputs with 0 `input[type="password"]` elements, preventing browser credential managers from storing or sniffing AI keys.
+++- **model_allowlist:** An explicit allowlist of model names permitted for execution under this provider configuration (e.g. `["gemini-2.5-flash", "gemini-2.5-pro"]` or `["llama3.2:latest"]`). Administrators can manually specify identifiers or click `Probe Models` to automatically query the upstream provider API and select discovered models via interactive search chips.
+++- **timeout_ms:** The maximum wall-clock request duration allowed for provider calls, bounded between 1,000 ms and 300,000 ms (default 30,000 ms, with a 300-second reasoning ceiling). When combined with agent execution, the runtime enforces `min(provider.timeout_ms, agent.timeout_ms)`. If the upstream model exceeds this cutoff, the system aborts execution, fails closed, and records `GATE_TIMEOUT` without hanging worker threads.
+++- **temperature:** Governs generation randomness on a scale from 0.0 to 2.0 (default 0.7). Lower temperatures (0.1 to 0.3) provide highly deterministic outputs recommended for code review, transition gates, and structured JSON parsing, while moderate values (0.7 to 1.0) support creative sticky synthesis and brainstorming.
+++- **top_p:** Controls nucleus sampling by restricting generation to tokens comprising the top P probability mass (0.0 to 1.0, default 1.0). Decreasing this value eliminates low-probability generation branches, enhancing predictability in mission-critical QA workflows.
+++- **max_tokens:** The upper bound on token output generated in a single completion or reasoning turn (configurable from 1 to 128,000 tokens, default 4,096). Protects against unbounded model loops and limits context window consumption.
+++- **mtls_cert_pem:** An optional client certificate in PEM format used when establishing mutual TLS connections to enterprise AI gateways or private proxy infrastructure.
+++- **is_enabled:** A boolean toggle that immediately activates or deactivates the provider without deleting its configuration or keys. Inactive providers are bypassed during live completion dispatch and sparkle routing.
+++- **priority_and_order:** The failover dispatch sequence determined via `POST /api/v1/admin/ai/providers/order` (or `PUT /api/v1/workspaces/:ws/ai-providers/order`). Providers are evaluated sequentially by priority. If a higher-tier provider encounters a 5xx error or rate limit, the router seamlessly falls back to the next active provider in the stack.
+++- **token_cap_and_budgets:** Managed via `POST /api/v1/admin/ai/providers/budgets` (or `PUT /api/v1/workspaces/:ws/ai-providers/budgets`) across calendar monthly periods. Setting `token_cap = 0` triggers a Hard AI Freeze, immediately rejecting reservations with `BudgetExceeded` and halting Sparkle completions with status `budget`. Selecting Unlimited Quota configures an internal ceiling of 10,000,000,000 tokens, recording ledger telemetry without automated cutoffs. Quick presets provide standard allowances of 1M, 5M, 25M, and 100M tokens.
