+-    "content": "# [Guide] Multi-Provider AI Engine, Execution Harnesses, and Runners\n\n## Overview\n\nUnlike commercial SaaS ticketing platforms that hardcode proprietary cloud models behind expensive monthly add-ons, \ud83d\udc4dkanban features an open, multi-provider **AI Orchestration Engine** (`agent/`). Organizations can leverage cloud AI providers, local open-source models, or zero-shell isolated command-line pipes without changing application code.\n\nSupported provider ecosystems:\n- **Cloud LLMs:** OpenAI (GPT-4o, o3-mini), Google Gemini (Gemini 2.5 Flash/Pro), and OpenAI-compatible gateways.\n- **Local Private LLMs:** Ollama (Llama 3, DeepSeek-R1, Qwen 2.5) running completely on-premise with zero data egress.\n- **Zero-Shell CLI Pipe Harnesses:** Direct pipe execution (`agy -p`, `codex -p`) running inside hardened Linux sandboxes.\n- **Remote Read-Only mTLS Runners:** Distributed runners executing builds and code inspections over encrypted WebSockets.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph Core[\"\ud83d\udc4dkanban Agent Runtime\"]\n        App[\"FastAPI & Worker Dispatcher\"]\n        Protocol[\"LLMProvider Interface (agent/providers/base.py)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Providers[\"Multi-Provider Implementations\"]\n        OpenAI[\"OpenAI Provider<br/>GPT-4o, o3-mini, compatible gateways\"]\n        Gemini[\"Google Gemini Provider<br/>Gemini 2.5 Flash / Pro (native tools)\"]\n        Ollama[\"Local Ollama Provider<br/>Llama 3, DeepSeek-R1, Qwen 2.5\"]\n        CLIPipe[\"Zero-Shell CLI Pipe<br/>agy -p / codex -p in nsjail\"]\n        Runner[\"Remote mTLS Runner<br/>Encrypted WSS, read-only repo\"]\n    end\n\n    App --> Protocol\n    Protocol --> OpenAI\n    Protocol --> Gemini\n    Protocol --> Ollama\n    Protocol --> CLIPipe\n    Protocol --> Runner\n```\n\n![Async Agent Gate Card States and Transition Lifecycle](images/16-async-agent-gate-states.png)\n\n---\n\n## Provider Architecture and Abstraction Layer\n\nThe engine abstracts all AI interactions behind a clean, asynchronous provider protocol (`agent/providers/base.py`):\n\n```python\nclass LLMProvider(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    async def complete(self, prompt: str, system: str | None = None, **kwargs) -> CompletionResult:\n        \"\"\"Execute a text completion or FIM generation.\"\"\"\n\n    @abstractmethod\n    async def chat(self, messages: list[ChatMessage], **kwargs) -> ChatResult:\n        \"\"\"Execute a multi-turn conversation with tool calling.\"\"\"\n\n    @abstractmethod\n    async def embed(self, texts: list[str]) -> list[list[float]]:\n        \"\"\"Generate vector embeddings for semantic search.\"\"\"\n```\n\n<a id=\"supported-runtimes\"></a>\n### Supported Providers Matrix\n\n| Provider Key | Primary Use Cases | Streaming Support | Function / Tool Calling | Network Boundary |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| **`openai`** | Complex planning, synthesis | Yes | Yes (Native JSON Schema) | `egress_llm_net` |\n| **`gemini`** | Large context specs, vision | Yes | Yes (Native Function Calling) | `egress_llm_net` |\n| **`ollama`** | Air-gapped workstations, privacy | Yes | Yes (Model dependent) | `data_net` / local |\n| **`cli_pipe`** | Deterministic CI agents, bash-less | No (Batch) | Pipe JSON protocol | `sandbox_net` (nsjail) |\n| **`remote_runner`** | Forensic code audits, test gates | Yes (WSS) | Streamable RPC | Dedicated mTLS |\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"execution-harnesses\"></a>\n## Zero-Shell CLI Pipe Harnesses (`agy -p` / `codex -p`)\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant Engine as AI Orchestrator\n    participant Sandbox as nsjail Sandbox Runner\n    participant Model as CLI Pipe (agy -p / codex -p)\n\n    Engine->>Sandbox: Spawn isolated container (CLONE_NEWNET, read-only FS, 512MB RAM)\n    Engine->>Sandbox: Pipe prompt.json over STDIN\n    Sandbox->>Model: Execute deterministic harness without shell/bash\n    Model->>Sandbox: Generate tool call / response JSON over STDOUT\n    Sandbox-->>Engine: Structured JSON stream (Zero bash exposure)\n    Engine->>Engine: Validate response against Pydantic schema\n```\n\nAutonomous agents frequently need to inspect code, run validators, or check commit history. However, granting an LLM arbitrary bash or shell access introduces severe remote code execution (RCE) hazards.\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban resolves this with **Zero-Shell CLI Pipes**:\n- Agents do not receive an interactive shell or bash process.\n- Execution communicates strictly over standard input (`stdin`) and standard output (`stdout`) using structured JSON pipes.\n- Commands run inside a sandboxed Linux jail (`nsjail` / `setpriv`):\n  - Read-only root filesystem.\n  - Network namespace disabled (`CLONE_NEWNET`).\n  - Memory limit capped at 512 MB.\n  - Execution time limited to 15 seconds.\n\n```bash\n# Example invocation of pipe harness:\ncat prompt.json | agy -p --sandbox-jail > response.json\n```\n\n---\n\n## AI Providers and Token Caps Studio (`/admin/ai`)\n\nWorkspace and server administrators manage LLM provider registrations, write-only API key custody, monthly token budgets, and zero-egress policies in the visual studio at `/admin/ai`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph AIStudio[\"AI Providers & Token Caps Architecture\"]\n        AdminUser[\"Administrator (AAL2)\"] --> Reg[\"Register / Edit Provider\"]\n        Reg --> ProvidersList[\"Configured Providers<br/>- OpenAI (Cloud, priority 10)<br/>- Gemini (Cloud, priority 20)<br/>- Ollama (Local Air-Gap, priority 5)\"]\n        \n        ProvidersList --> AirGapCheck{\"Air-Gap Policy Active?\"}\n        AirGapCheck -->|Yes| BlockCloud[\"Cloud Providers Blocked<br/>Only Local Zero-Egress Ollama Allowed\"]\n        AirGapCheck -->|No| AllowCloud[\"Dispatched by Priority Tier\"]\n        \n        AllowCloud --> BudgetGate{\"Monthly Token Budget Gate\"}\n        BlockCloud --> BudgetGate\n        \n        BudgetGate -->|Within Cap| Exec[\"Execute Completion / Tool Call\"]\n        BudgetGate -->|Cap Exceeded| Reject[\"429 Budget Exceeded<br/>Alert Admin to Reset or Expand Cap\"]\n    end\n```\n\n![AI Providers and Token Caps Studio](images/43-admin-ai-studio-providers.png)\n\n### Key Provider Governance Features:\n1. **Write-Only API Key Custody:** API keys are submitted over encrypted TLS, stored encrypted in the database, and never echoed back to the browser.\n2. **Enterprise Air-Gap Mode:** When activated, all cloud egress to external AI services is blocked at the network layer. Only local, on-premise runtimes (such as Ollama) are authorized for completions.\n3. **Monthly Token Budgets & Ledgers:** Each provider configuration enforces monthly input/output token caps with automated rollover and audit ledger accounting.\n\n---\n\n## Agent Definitions Studio (`/admin/agents`)\n\nAdministrators define and customize autonomous agent personas, tool permissions, and system prompt templates at `/admin/agents`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph AgentDefFlow[\"Agent Definition & Validation Lifecycle\"]\n        Def[\"Agent Definition<br/>Name, Slug, Model, Prompt Template\"] --> Scope[\"Tool Allowlist Scoping<br/>Permitted MCP Tools (read_ticket, add_comment, etc.)\"]\n        Scope --> Modal[\"Validation Modal (/admin/agents)<br/>Simulate test prompt against agent schema\"]\n        Modal --> Verdict{\"Validation Passes?\"}\n        Verdict -->|Yes| Active[\"Registered as Active Agent Principal\"]\n        Verdict -->|No| Fix[\"Report Schema / Prompt Errors for Correction\"]\n    end\n```\n\n![Agent Definitions Studio Overview](images/44-admin-agent-definitions.png)\n\n![Agent Validation Modal](images/60-agent-validation-modal.png)\n\n### Definition Capabilities:\n- **Role and Model Mapping:** Bind specific personas (e.g. `reality-checker`, `security-auditor`, `code-reviewer`) to optimal models (Gemini 2.5 Flash, GPT-4o, DeepSeek-R1).\n- **Tool Allowlist Scoping:** Strictly restrict which MCP tools each agent principal can invoke, enforcing least privilege.\n- **Interactive Validation Modal:** Test and validate agent definitions before deploying them to live project workflows.\n\n---\n\n## Agent Runners Studio and Distributed mTLS Daemon (`/admin/agent-runners`)\n\nFor heavy build verification, sandbox test execution, and isolated code review, \ud83d\udc4dkanban deploys distributed **Agent Runners** managed through `/admin/agent-runners`.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    actor Admin as Server Administrator\n    participant Studio as Runner Studio (/admin/agent-runners)\n    participant Server as \ud83d\udc4dkanban Orchestrator\n    participant Daemon as Remote Runner Daemon\n\n    Admin->>Studio: Click \"+ Mint Runner Token\"\n    Studio->>Server: Generate single-use enrollment token (Max 15 min TTL)\n    Server-->>Studio: Return copyable CLI enrollment snippet\n    Admin->>Daemon: Execute enrollment on runner host\n    Daemon->>Server: Connect to dedicated mTLS port :8444 with PoP handshake\n    Server->>Server: Verify token & bind X.509 client certificate\n    Server-->>Daemon: Channel verified: Runner enters \"ONLINE\"\n    Daemon->>Server: Heartbeat ping every 15s over :8444 (CPU, RAM, active jobs)\n```\n\n![Agent Runner Studio Overview](images/45-admin-runner-studio.png)\n\n![Runner Mint Token Modal](images/61-runner-mint-token-modal.png)\n\n### Runner Infrastructure and Protocol Security\n\n- **Dedicated Port `:8444` and Proof-of-Possession Handshake:** Distributed runners connect exclusively over dedicated mutual TLS port `:8444`. Port `:8444` rejects standard HTTP requests and unauthenticated TLS handshakes, demanding a valid X.509 client certificate verified via a cryptographic Proof-of-Possession (PoP) challenge.\n- **Single-Use Enrollment Token (Max 15-Minute TTL):** When an administrator clicks `+ Mint Runner Token` in `/admin/agent-runners` (requiring AAL2 step-up authentication), the orchestrator mints an ephemeral enrollment token valid for at most 15 minutes (900 seconds). Upon first enrollment or expiration, the token is permanently invalidated.\n- **CLI Enrollment Syntax:** The studio generates an exact command to enroll the host daemon:\n  ```bash\n  echo \"${ENROLLMENT_TOKEN}\" | uv run kanban-agent-runner enroll \\\n    --server-url https://kanban.example.com \\\n    --token-stdin \\\n    --pool default\n  ```\n- **Hardware-Bound Certificate Rotation Guidance:** Runner client certificates are hardware-bound to the host and cannot be renewed in place. To rotate a certificate or decommission a host:\n  1. Revoke the existing runner in `/admin/agent-runners` (AAL2 challenge confirmed).\n  2. Mint a fresh enrollment token (15-minute TTL).\n  3. Re-enroll the runner daemon using the CLI command above.\n- **Private `/internal/v1/mcp/*` Runner Ingress Isolation:** All daemon polling, task dispatch, and execution telemetry endpoints (`/internal/v1/mcp/*`) are strictly segregated from public edge networks. They are reachable only over the internal container network via authenticated mTLS.\n\n---\n\n## Agent Revocation and Security Controls\n\nWhen an agent session exhibits anomalous activity or a credential leak is detected, administrators can invoke an instant Kill Switch across all surfaces:\n\n![Agent Kill Switch and Immediate Session Revocation](images/18-agent-kill-switch.png)\n\n---\n\n## Remote mTLS Repository Runners\n\nFor long-running verification tasks (e.g. compiling large C++ or Rust binaries, running full end-to-end Playwright suites), tasks are dispatched to external **mTLS Runners**:\n- **Mutual TLS Authentication (mTLS):** Both the \ud83d\udc4dkanban server and the runner verify cryptographic X.509 certificates over dedicated port `:8444`.\n- **WebSocket Streaming:** Real-time log output and build artifacts stream back over encrypted WebSocket channels.\n- **Read-Only Volume Mounting:** The repository is mounted read-only to prevent unauthorized code mutations on the build host.\n\n---\n\n## Testable AI: Pluggable Fixtures and CLI Validation\n\nAI agent workflows can be notoriously difficult to test deterministically in CI pipelines due to model drift, token costs, and rate limits. \ud83d\udc4dkanban enforces **100% Deterministic AI Testing**:\n\n### Pluggable AI Response Fixtures\nIn test suites, providers are swapped with `FixtureLLMProvider` and `FixtureCLIRunner`:\n- Replays recorded, deterministic JSON responses without making network calls.\n- Simulates network timeouts, rate limit errors (`429`), and malformed responses to verify system resilience.\n\n### Local Agent CLI Diagnostics\nEngineers can validate transition helpers and AI workflows locally via the command line:\n\n```bash\n# Validate a transition helper against a ticket fixture\nkanban-agent validate-transition \\\n  --ticket-file test_ticket.json \\\n  --from-state in_progress \\\n  --to-state done \\\n  --dry-run\n```\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Provider Benchmark & Cutoff Timeout Guide\n\nEvery AI provider configuration and agent definition exposes a configurable hard cutoff timeout (`timeout_ms`, `1000` to `300000` ms). At runtime, `GateWorkerRunner` enforces `cutoff_ms = min(provider.timeout_ms, agent.timeout_ms)`. When an upstream model exceeds `cutoff_ms`, the worker raises `GATE_TIMEOUT` and **fails closed**, leaving the ticket in its source state with zero hung workers.\n\n### Live Full-Stack In-Transition Benchmark Matrix (`In Progress -> Done`)\n\nMeasured end-to-end against `kanban-liveqa-2` (embedded PostgreSQL 16 + `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`) using `StrictVerdictSchema` (`kb-transition-verdict/v1`) on a Positive software ticket (`KAN-1`) and a Negative impossible/unverified ticket (`KAN-2: \"Build a rocket ship and serve ice cream on Saturn\"`):\n\n| Runtime & Model | Cutoff (`timeout_ms`) | Positive (`KAN-1`) | Positive Latency | Negative (`KAN-2`) | Negative Latency | Operational Guidance |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :---: | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| **Google Gemini** (`gemini-2.5-flash-lite`) | `20000` | `approve` (`Done`) | `3,215 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `1,594 ms` | Fastest overall cloud model with strict schema compliance and `v1beta` AI Studio routing. |\n| **OpenAI Cloud** (`gpt-4o-mini`) | `20000` | `approve` (`Done`) | `3,846 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `2,224 ms` | Fast cloud fallback with strict JSON schema adherence. |\n| **Local Ollama Supercomputer** (`gemma4:e2b`) | `25000` | `approve` (`Done`) | `19,892 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `5,213 ms` | Strong air-gapped 2B edge choice; `20000`-`25000 ms` cutoff recommended. |\n| **Local Ollama GPU/CPU** (`gemma3:4b`) | `20000` / `35000` | `timed_out` (20s) / `approve` (35s) | `20,929 ms` / `31,431 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `16,630 ms` | If VRAM is shared with another pinned model (CPU fallback), set `timeout_ms >= 35000`. |\n| **Local Ollama GPU/CPU** (`granite3.3:2b`) | `20000` | `timed_out` (fail-closed) | `20,321 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `12,691 ms` | Warm-keep or `25000 ms` cutoff avoids cold-load timeout on long prompts. |\n| **Local Ollama Reasoning** (`nemotron-3-super:latest`) | `5000` (Strict) | N/A | N/A | `GATE_TIMEOUT` (fail-closed) | `6,023 ms` | Validates hard 5s cutoff protection with clean fail-closed veto. |\n| **Local Ollama Reasoning** (`nemotron-3-super:latest`) | `45000` (Extended) | `approve` (`Done`) | `31,940 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `26,502 ms` | Heavy reasoning models (`<think>` tags stripped) should use `45000`-`60000 ms`. |\n\n### Choosing a Cutoff Timeout\n- **Fast Cloud Models (`gpt-4o-mini`, `gemini-2.5-flash-lite`):** `15000` to `20000` ms.\n- **Local 2B-4B Edge Models (`gemma4:e2b`, `gemma3:4b`, `granite3.3:2b`):** `25000` to `35000` ms (covers occasional model reload or shared VRAM).\n- **Heavy Reasoning Models (`nemotron-3-super:latest`, `deepseek-r1`):** `45000` to `60000` ms, or pair with a strict `5000`-`10000` ms cutoff on latency-sensitive columns so stuck calls fail closed immediately.\n\n<a id=\"remote-runners\"></a>\n<a id=\"repository-access\"></a>\n"
++    "content": "# [Guide] Multi-Provider AI Engine, Execution Harnesses, and Runners\n\n## Overview\n\nUnlike commercial SaaS ticketing platforms that hardcode proprietary cloud models behind expensive monthly add-ons, \ud83d\udc4dkanban features an open, multi-provider **AI Orchestration Engine** (`agent/`). Organizations can leverage cloud AI providers, local open-source models, or zero-shell isolated command-line pipes without changing application code.\n\nSupported provider ecosystems:\n- **Cloud LLMs:** Google Gemini (Gemini 2.5 Flash, Gemini 2.5 Pro), OpenAI (GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini), and OpenAI-compatible gateways.\n- **Local Private LLMs:** Ollama (Llama 3, DeepSeek-R1, Qwen 2.5) running completely on-premise with zero data egress.\n- **Zero-Shell CLI Pipe Harnesses:** Direct pipe execution (`agy -p`, `codex -p`) running inside hardened Linux sandboxes.\n- **Remote Read-Only mTLS Runners:** Distributed runners executing builds and code inspections over encrypted WebSockets.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph Core[\"\ud83d\udc4dkanban Agent Runtime\"]\n        App[\"FastAPI & Worker Dispatcher\"]\n        Protocol[\"LLMProvider Interface (agent/providers/base.py)\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Providers[\"Multi-Provider Implementations\"]\n        OpenAI[\"OpenAI Provider - GPT-4o, o3-mini, compatible gateways\"]\n        Gemini[\"Google Gemini Provider - Gemini 2.5 Flash / Pro (native tools)\"]\n        Ollama[\"Local Ollama Provider - Llama 3, DeepSeek-R1, Qwen 2.5\"]\n        CLIPipe[\"Zero-Shell CLI Pipe - agy -p / codex -p in nsjail\"]\n        Runner[\"Remote mTLS Runner - Encrypted WSS, read-only repo\"]\n    end\n\n    App --> Protocol\n    Protocol --> OpenAI\n    Protocol --> Gemini\n    Protocol --> Ollama\n    Protocol --> CLIPipe\n    Protocol --> Runner\n```\n\n![Async Agent Gate Card States and Transition Lifecycle](images/16-async-agent-gate-states.png)\n\n---\n\n## Provider Architecture and Abstraction Layer\n\nThe engine abstracts all AI interactions behind a clean, asynchronous provider protocol (`agent/providers/base.py`):\n\n```python\nclass LLMProvider(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    async def complete(self, prompt: str, system: str | None = None, **kwargs) -> CompletionResult:\n        \"\"\"Execute a text completion or FIM generation.\"\"\"\n\n    @abstractmethod\n    async def chat(self, messages: list[ChatMessage], **kwargs) -> ChatResult:\n        \"\"\"Execute a multi-turn conversation with tool calling.\"\"\"\n\n    @abstractmethod\n    async def embed(self, texts: list[str]) -> list[list[float]]:\n        \"\"\"Generate vector embeddings for semantic search.\"\"\"\n```\n\n## Supported Runtimes\n\nThe platform provides first-class support for cloud, local air-gapped, CLI-piped, and distributed runner runtimes:\n\n| Provider Key | Primary Use Cases | Streaming Support | Function / Tool Calling | Network Boundary |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| `gemini` | Large context specs, vision, reasoning | Yes | Yes (Native Function Calling) | `egress_llm_net` |\n| `openai` | Complex planning, synthesis, code review | Yes | Yes (Native JSON Schema) | `egress_llm_net` |\n| `ollama` | Air-gapped workstations, strict data privacy | Yes | Yes (Model dependent) | `data_net` / local |\n| `cli_pipe` | Deterministic CI agents, bash-less execution | No (Batch) | Pipe JSON protocol | `sandbox_net` (nsjail) |\n| `remote_runner` | Forensic code audits, Playwright test gates | Yes (WSS) | Streamable RPC | Dedicated mTLS |\n\n---\n\n## Execution Harnesses\n\nAutonomous agents frequently need to inspect code, run validators, or check commit history. However, granting an LLM arbitrary bash or shell access introduces severe remote code execution (RCE) hazards.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant Engine as AI Orchestrator\n    participant Sandbox as nsjail Sandbox Runner\n    participant Model as CLI Pipe (agy -p / codex -p)\n\n    Engine->>Sandbox: Spawn isolated container (CLONE_NEWNET, read-only FS, 512MB RAM)\n    Engine->>Sandbox: Pipe prompt.json over STDIN\n    Sandbox->>Model: Execute deterministic harness without shell/bash\n    Model->>Sandbox: Generate tool call / response JSON over STDOUT\n    Sandbox-->>Engine: Structured JSON stream (Zero bash exposure)\n    Engine->>Engine: Validate response against Pydantic schema\n```\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban resolves this with **Zero-Shell CLI Pipes**:\n- Agents do not receive an interactive shell or bash process.\n- Execution communicates strictly over standard input (`stdin`) and standard output (`stdout`) using structured JSON pipes.\n- Commands run inside a sandboxed Linux jail (`nsjail` / `setpriv`):\n  - Read-only root filesystem.\n  - Network namespace disabled (`CLONE_NEWNET`).\n  - Memory limit capped at 512 MB.\n  - Execution time limited to 15 seconds.\n\n```bash\n# Example invocation of pipe harness:\ncat prompt.json | agy -p --sandbox-jail > response.json\n```\n\n---\n\n## AI Providers and Token Caps Studio\n\nWorkspace and server administrators manage LLM provider registrations, write-only API key custody, monthly token budgets, and zero-egress policies in the visual studio at `/admin/ai`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph AIStudio[\"AI Providers & Token Caps Architecture\"]\n        AdminUser[\"Administrator (AAL2)\"] --> Reg[\"Register / Edit Provider\"]\n        Reg --> ProvidersList[\"Configured Providers - OpenAI (Cloud, priority 10), Gemini (Cloud, priority 20), Ollama (Local Air-Gap, priority 5)\"]\n        \n        ProvidersList --> AirGapCheck{\"Air-Gap Policy Active?\"}\n        AirGapCheck -->|Yes| BlockCloud[\"Cloud Providers Blocked - Only Local Zero-Egress Ollama Allowed\"]\n        AirGapCheck -->|No| AllowCloud[\"Dispatched by Priority Tier\"]\n        \n        AllowCloud --> BudgetGate{\"Monthly Token Budget Gate\"}\n        BlockCloud --> BudgetGate\n        \n        BudgetGate -->|Within Cap| Exec[\"Execute Completion / Tool Call\"]\n        BudgetGate -->|Cap Exceeded| Reject[\"429 Budget Exceeded - Alert Admin to Reset or Expand Cap\"]\n    end\n```\n\n![AI Providers Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice2-ai-studio-1440x900-light.png)\n\n![AI Providers Studio - Desktop Dark](images/slice2-ai-studio-1440x900-dark.png)\n\n![AI Providers Studio - Tablet Landscape](images/slice2-ai-studio-1024x768-tablet-landscape.png)\n\n![AI Providers Studio - Tablet Portrait](images/slice2-ai-studio-768x1024-tablet-portrait.png)\n\n![AI Providers Studio - Mobile Portrait](images/slice2-ai-studio-390x844-mobile-portrait.png)\n\n### Key Provider Governance Features:\n1. **Write-Only API Key Custody:** API keys are submitted over encrypted TLS, stored encrypted in the database using envelope encryption, and never echoed back to the browser. The editor drawer contains 0 password inputs (`input[type=\"password\"]`), completely eliminating DOM password sniffing.\n2. **Enterprise Air-Gap Mode:** When activated (`air_gap_mode: true`), all cloud egress to external AI services is blocked at the network layer. Only local, on-premise runtimes (such as Ollama) are authorized for completions. Cloud provider creation and probing attempts return HTTP 409 `AIR_GAP_EGRESS_DENIED`.\n3. **Monthly Token Budgets & Ledgers:** Each provider configuration enforces monthly input/output token caps with automated rollover and audit ledger accounting.\n\n---\n\n## Budget Caps\n\nThe Token Budget Modal (`AiBudgetModal.tsx`) provides granular control over organizational spending across each configured AI provider.\n\n![Token Budget Modal - Unlimited Quota](images/slice2-ai-budget-modal-unlimited.png)\n\n![Token Budget Modal - Hard AI Freeze](images/slice2-ai-budget-modal-freeze.png)\n\n### Token Cap Policies:\n- **Enforced Cap** (`cap-mode-enforce-btn`): Administrators define a monthly token quota (e.g. 1,000,000, 5,000,000, 25,000,000, or 100,000,000 tokens via quick presets). Once `tokens_used >= token_cap`, subsequent reservations fail closed with `BudgetExceeded`, and Sparkle FIM completions return status `budget`.\n- **Hard AI Freeze** (`preset-budget-0-freeze-btn`): Setting `token_cap = 0` immediately halts all model queries and agent transition checks for that provider. It serves as an instant financial circuit breaker.\n- **Unlimited Quota** (`cap-mode-unlimited-btn`): Sets the internal ceiling to `10_000_000_000` tokens, tracking ledger consumption without automated cutoffs.\n\n---\n\n## Sampling Parameters\n\nSampling parameters calibrate model behavior across deterministic verification gates versus open-ended synthesis:\n\n| Parameter | UI Control | Range / Allowed Values | Default | Purpose & Invariant |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `temperature` | `provider-temperature-input` | `0.0` to `2.0` | `0.7` | Governs randomness. Use low values (`0.1` to `0.3`) for deterministic code generation, test gates, and QA verdicts. |\n| `top_p` | `provider-top-p-input` | `0.0` to `1.0` | `1.0` | Nucleus sampling threshold pruning low-probability tokens from generation. |\n| `max_tokens` | `provider-max-tokens-input` | `1` to `128000` | `4096` | Upper bound on generation tokens for completion and reasoning traces. |\n| `timeout_ms` | `provider-timeout-ms-input` | `1000` to `300000` ms | `30000` ms | Hard ceiling on provider HTTP requests. Maximum 300s reasoning ceiling. |\n| `mtls_cert_pem` | `provider-mtls-cert-input` | PEM Certificate | Empty | Optional client certificate for mutual TLS provider connections. |\n\n---\n\n## Agent Definitions Studio\n\nAdministrators author and manage autonomous agent personas, tool permissions, and system prompt templates at `/admin/agents`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph AgentDefFlow[\"Agent Definition & Validation Lifecycle\"]\n        Def[\"Agent Definition - Name, Slug, Model, Prompt Template\"] --> Scope[\"Tool Allowlist Scoping - Permitted MCP Tools (read_ticket, add_comment, etc.)\"]\n        Scope --> Modal[\"Validation Modal (/admin/agents) - Simulate test prompt against agent schema\"]\n        Modal --> Verdict{\"Validation Passes?\"}\n        Verdict -->|Yes| Active[\"Registered as Active Agent Principal\"]\n        Verdict -->|No| Fix[\"Report Schema / Prompt Errors for Correction\"]\n    end\n```\n\n![Agent Definitions Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice2-agent-studio-1440x900-light.png)\n\n![Agent Definitions Studio - Desktop Dark](images/slice2-agent-studio-1440x900-dark.png)\n\n![Agent Definitions Studio - Tablet Landscape](images/slice2-agent-studio-1024x768-tablet-landscape.png)\n\n![Agent Definitions Studio - Tablet Portrait](images/slice2-agent-studio-768x1024-tablet-portrait.png)\n\n![Agent Definitions Studio - Mobile Portrait](images/slice2-agent-studio-390x844-mobile-portrait.png)\n\n![Agent Definitions Studio Overview](images/44-admin-agent-definitions.png)\n\n### Definition Capabilities:\n- **Role and Model Mapping:** Bind specific personas (such as `reality-checker`, `security-auditor`, `code-reviewer`) to optimal models (Gemini 2.5 Flash, GPT-4o, DeepSeek-R1).\n- **Harness Selection:** Select `in_app_sdk` (in-process LLM calling), `cli_pipe` (sandboxed zero-shell command pipe), or `remote_runner` (distributed mTLS build worker).\n- **Tool Allowlist Scoping:** Strictly restrict which MCP tools each agent principal can invoke, enforcing least privilege (`search_tickets`, `read_ticket`, `search_knowledge`, `read_doc`, `add_comment`).\n- **Interactive Validation Modal:** Test and validate agent definitions before deploying them to live project workflows.\n\n![Agent Validation Modal](images/60-agent-validation-modal.png)\n\n![Agent Validation Modal - Forensic Trace](images/slice2-agent-validation-modal-trace.png)\n\n---\n\n## Repository Access\n\nWhen configuring autonomous agents in `AgentDefinitionEditorDrawer.tsx`, administrators select the repository access mode granted to the agent during execution:\n\n| Mode | Allowed Values | Default | Operational & Security Boundary |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `none` | Zero filesystem access | `none` | Agent operates purely on ticket metadata, comments, and in-app documents without cloning or mounting code. |\n| `ro_vfs_mount` | Read-Only VFS Mount | None | Mounts the Git repository strictly read-only within the execution container (`CLONE_NEWNS`). Ideal for forensic QA and static code review. |\n| `bare_git_tools` | Bare Git Read-Only Tools | None | Provides read-only bare Git inspection tools in an isolated tmpfs directory. Prevents repo modification while permitting fast commit, diff, and tree inspection. |\n\n---\n\n## Remote Runners\n\nFor heavy build verification, sandbox test execution, and isolated code review, \ud83d\udc4dkanban deploys distributed **Remote Runners** managed through `/admin/agent-runners`.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    actor Admin as Server Administrator\n    participant Studio as Runner Studio (/admin/agent-runners)\n    participant Server as \ud83d\udc4dkanban Orchestrator\n    participant Daemon as Remote Runner Daemon\n\n    Admin->>Studio: Click \"+ Mint Runner Token\"\n    Studio->>Server: Generate single-use enrollment token (Max 15 min TTL)\n    Server-->>Studio: Return copyable CLI enrollment snippet\n    Admin->>Daemon: Execute enrollment on runner host\n    Daemon->>Server: Connect to dedicated mTLS port :8444 with PoP handshake\n    Server->>Server: Verify token & bind X.509 client certificate\n    Server-->>Daemon: Channel verified: Runner enters \"ONLINE\"\n    Daemon->>Server: Heartbeat ping every 15s over :8444 (CPU, RAM, active jobs)\n```\n\n![Remote Runners Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice2-runner-studio-1440x900-light.png)\n\n![Remote Runners Studio - Desktop Dark](images/slice2-runner-studio-1440x900-dark.png)\n\n![Remote Runners Studio - Tablet Landscape](images/slice2-runner-studio-1024x768-tablet-landscape.png)\n\n![Remote Runners Studio - Tablet Portrait](images/slice2-runner-studio-768x1024-tablet-portrait.png)\n\n![Remote Runners Studio - Mobile Portrait](images/slice2-runner-studio-390x844-mobile-portrait.png)\n\n![Agent Runner Studio Overview](images/45-admin-runner-studio.png)\n\n![Runner Mint Token Modal](images/61-runner-mint-token-modal.png)\n\n![Runner Mint Token Modal - Masked](images/slice2-runner-mint-token-modal.png)\n\n### Runner Infrastructure and Protocol Security\n\n- **Dedicated Port :8444 and Proof-of-Possession Handshake:** Distributed runners connect exclusively over dedicated mutual TLS port `:8444`. Port `:8444` rejects standard HTTP requests and unauthenticated TLS handshakes, demanding a valid X.509 client certificate verified via a cryptographic Proof-of-Possession (PoP) challenge format: `kb-runner-pop-v1:{runner_id}:{nonce}:{cert_sha256}`.\n- **Single-Use Enrollment Token (Max 15-Minute TTL):** When an administrator clicks `+ Mint Runner Token` in `/admin/agent-runners` (requiring AAL2 step-up authentication), the orchestrator mints an ephemeral enrollment token valid for at most 15 minutes (900 seconds). Upon first enrollment or expiration, the token is permanently invalidated.\n- **Unprivileged Non-Root Daemon Execution:** The daemon runs as a standard unprivileged user. Generated key material enforces strict POSIX file permissions:\n  - State directory: `0o700` (`drwx------`)\n  - Private key (`client.key`): `0o600` (`-rw-------`)\n  - CA certificate (`ca.pem`): `0o600` (`-rw-------`)\n  - Client certificate (`client.crt`): `0o644` (`-rw-r--r--`)\n- **CLI Enrollment Syntax:** The studio generates an exact command to enroll the host daemon:\n  ```bash\n  echo \"$TOKEN\" | uv run kanban-agent-runner enroll \\\n    --server-url https://kanban.example.com \\\n    --token-stdin \\\n    --pool default\n  ```\n- **Hardware-Bound Certificate Rotation Guidance:** Runner client certificates are hardware-bound to the host and cannot be renewed in place. To rotate a certificate or decommission a host:\n  1. Revoke the existing runner in `/admin/agent-runners` via `POST /api/v1/admin/agent-runners/{id}:revoke`.\n  2. Mint a fresh enrollment token (15-minute TTL).\n  3. Re-enroll the runner daemon using the CLI command above.\n- **Private Ingress Isolation:** All daemon polling, task dispatch, and execution telemetry endpoints are strictly segregated from public edge networks.\n\n---\n\n## Agent Revocation and Security Controls\n\nWhen an agent session exhibits anomalous activity or a credential leak is detected, administrators can invoke an instant Kill Switch across all surfaces:\n\n![Agent Kill Switch and Immediate Session Revocation](images/18-agent-kill-switch.png)\n\n---\n\n## Testable AI: Pluggable Fixtures and CLI Validation\n\nAI agent workflows can be notoriously difficult to test deterministically in CI pipelines due to model drift, token costs, and rate limits. \ud83d\udc4dkanban enforces **100% Deterministic AI Testing**:\n\n### Pluggable AI Response Fixtures\nIn test suites, providers are swapped with `FixtureLLMProvider` and `FixtureCLIRunner`:\n- Replays recorded, deterministic JSON responses without making network calls.\n- Simulates network timeouts, rate limit errors (`429`), and malformed responses to verify system resilience.\n\n### Local Agent CLI Diagnostics\nEngineers can validate transition helpers and AI workflows locally via the command line:\n\n```bash\n# Validate a transition helper against a ticket fixture\nkanban-agent validate-transition \\\n  --ticket-file test_ticket.json \\\n  --from-state in_progress \\\n  --to-state done \\\n  --dry-run\n```\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Provider Benchmark Cutoff Timeout Guide\n\nEvery AI provider configuration and agent definition exposes a configurable hard cutoff timeout (`timeout_ms`, `1000` to `300000` ms). At runtime, `GateWorkerRunner` enforces `cutoff_ms = min(provider.timeout_ms, agent.timeout_ms)`. When an upstream model exceeds `cutoff_ms`, the worker raises `GATE_TIMEOUT` and **fails closed**, leaving the ticket in its source state with zero hung workers.\n\n### Live Full-Stack In-Transition Benchmark Matrix (In Progress -> Done)\n\nMeasured end-to-end against `kanban-liveqa-2` (embedded PostgreSQL 16 + `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`) using `StrictVerdictSchema` (`kb-transition-verdict/v1`) on a Positive software ticket (`KAN-1`) and a Negative impossible/unverified ticket (`KAN-2: \"Build a rocket ship and serve ice cream on Saturn\"`):\n\n| Runtime & Model | Cutoff (`timeout_ms`) | Positive (`KAN-1`) | Positive Latency | Negative (`KAN-2`) | Negative Latency | Operational Guidance |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Google Gemini** (`gemini-2.5-flash-lite`) | `20000` | `approve` (`Done`) | `3,215 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `1,594 ms` | Fastest overall cloud model with strict schema compliance and `v1beta` AI Studio routing. |\n| **OpenAI Cloud** (`gpt-4o-mini`) | `20000` | `approve` (`Done`) | `3,846 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `2,224 ms` | Fast cloud fallback with strict JSON schema adherence. |\n| **Local Ollama Supercomputer** (`gemma4:e2b`) | `25000` | `approve` (`Done`) | `19,892 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `5,213 ms` | Strong air-gapped 2B edge choice; `20000`-`25000 ms` cutoff recommended. |\n| **Local Ollama GPU/CPU** (`gemma3:4b`) | `20000` / `35000` | `timed_out` (20s) / `approve` (35s) | `20,929 ms` / `31,431 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `16,630 ms` | If VRAM is shared with another pinned model (CPU fallback), set `timeout_ms >= 35000`. |\n| **Local Ollama GPU/CPU** (`granite3.3:2b`) | `20000` | `timed_out` (fail-closed) | `20,321 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `12,691 ms` | Warm-keep or `25000 ms` cutoff avoids cold-load timeout on long prompts. |\n| **Local Ollama Reasoning** (`nemotron-3-super:latest`) | `5000` (Strict) | N/A | N/A | `GATE_TIMEOUT` (fail-closed) | `6,023 ms` | Validates hard 5s cutoff protection with clean fail-closed veto. |\n| **Local Ollama Reasoning** (`nemotron-3-super:latest`) | `45000` (Extended) | `approve` (`Done`) | `31,940 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `26,502 ms` | Heavy reasoning models (`<think>` tags stripped) should use `45000`-`60000 ms`. |\n\n### Choosing a Cutoff Timeout\n- **Fast Cloud Models** (`gpt-4o-mini`, `gemini-2.5-flash-lite`): `15000` to `20000` ms.\n- **Local 2B-4B Edge Models** (`gemma4:e2b`, `gemma3:4b`, `granite3.3:2b`): `25000` to `35000` ms (covers occasional model reload or shared VRAM).\n- **Heavy Reasoning Models** (`nemotron-3-super:latest`, `deepseek-r1`): `45000` to `60000` ms, or pair with a strict `5000`-`10000` ms cutoff on latency-sensitive columns so stuck calls fail closed immediately.\n\n---\n\n## Step-by-Step Operational Guides\n\n### Guide A: Configuring Gemini, OpenAI and Air-Gapped Ollama Providers\n1. Open the AI Providers Studio at `/admin/ai` (or workspace settings at `/workspaces/{ws}/settings`).\n2. Click `+ Register Provider` (`register-provider-btn`) to open the `AiProviderEditorDrawer`.\n3. Choose the provider preset or set `provider_kind`: for Google Gemini, click `Gemini` preset (`preset-gemini-btn`), enter display name `Google Gemini Workspace`, base URL `https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com`, paste API key, click `Probe Models` (`probe-models-btn`) to populate the model list (`gemini-2.5-flash`, `gemini-2.5-pro`), and set timeout ceiling (`15000` to `20000` ms). For OpenAI / Compatible, click `OpenAI` preset (`preset-openai-btn`), enter display name, base URL `https://api.openai.com/v1`, paste API key, and probe or select models (`gpt-4o`, `gpt-4o-mini`). For Local Ollama, click `Ollama` preset (`preset-ollama-btn`), enter display name `Local Ollama Runtime`, base URL `http://127.0.0.1:11434`, residency tier `local_only`, and click `Probe Models` to discover locally hosted models (`llama3.2:latest`, `qwen2.5-coder:latest`). API key is optional for local runtimes.\n4. Expand `Advanced Settings` (`advanced-settings-details`) to adjust temperature (default `0.7`), top-p (default `1.0`), max tokens (default `4096`), and optional mTLS client certificates.\n5. Click `Save Provider` (`save-provider-btn`).\n6. Reorder failover priority stack using drag handles or Up/Down buttons (`move-up-btn-{id}`, `move-down-btn-{id}`). The system attempts providers in priority order, falling back cleanly on timeouts or HTTP 5xx errors.\n7. To enforce zero cloud egress, navigate to Workspace Settings and enable `air_gap_mode = true`. Outbound cloud connections to `openai` and `gemini` are immediately rejected with HTTP 409 `AIR_GAP_EGRESS_DENIED`, while local Ollama queries continue without interruption.\n\n### Guide B: Setting Monthly Token Caps and Budget Halting\n1. In `/admin/ai`, locate the target provider card and click `Token Budget` (`manage-budget-btn-{id}`) to open the `AiBudgetModal`.\n2. Select the budget mode: choose Set Token Cap (`cap-mode-enforce-btn`) with monthly period and an integer token allowance or preset (`1M`, `5M`, `25M`, `100M Tokens`); choose Unlimited Quota (`cap-mode-unlimited-btn`) for an internal cap of `10_000_000_000` tokens; or choose Hard AI Freeze (`preset-budget-0-freeze-btn`) to set monthly cap to `0`, immediately blocking all completion and agent transition requests with `BudgetExceeded` (Sparkle FIM completions return status `budget`).\n3. Click `Save Budget` (`save-budget-btn`).\n4. The studio displays real-time progress bars indicating token consumption against the configured ceiling.\n\n### Guide C: Managing Workspace and Project Autonomous Agents\n1. Navigate to Agent Definitions Studio at `/admin/agents` (or project settings `/projects/{proj}/settings/agents`).\n2. Click `+ Register Agent` (`register-agent-btn`) to open the `AgentDefinitionEditorDrawer`.\n3. Fill in agent metadata including slug (lowercase alphanumeric string with hyphens, e.g. `reality-checker`, `security-auditor`), display name, and harness kind (`in_app_sdk`, `cli_pipe`, or `remote_runner`).\n4. Configure harness specifics: for `in_app_sdk`, select `provider_kind` (`gemini`, `openai`, `ollama`) and `model_id`; for `cli_pipe`, select pre-approved binary (`agy` or `codex`) and CLI args (`[\"-p\"]`); for `remote_runner`, select target pool slug (`default`).\n5. Select `repo_access_mode` (`none`, `ro_vfs_mount`, `bare_git_tools`) and check allowed MCP tools (`search_tickets`, `read_ticket`, `search_knowledge`, `read_doc`, `add_comment`).\n6. Enter System Instructions in Markdown directing agent persona and verification rules.\n7. Set timeout (`timeout_ms`, default 60000ms, up to 300000ms / 300s).\n8. Click `Save Agent` (`save-agent-btn`).\n9. Click `Validate` (`validate-agent-btn-{id}`) to open the `AgentValidationModal`. The system executes a non-destructive test trace and renders a 4-section report (`=== 1. RENDERED CONTEXT ===`, `=== 2. TOOL TRACE ===`, `=== 3. LATENCY & TOKENS ===`, `=== 4. SCHEMA & POLICY STATUS ===`).\n\n### Guide D: Enrolling Non-Root mTLS Runners\n1. Navigate to Remote Runners Studio at `/admin/agent-runners`.\n2. Click `+ Mint Enrollment Token` (`open-mint-modal-btn`) to open the `MintEnrollmentTokenModal`.\n3. Select pool slug (`default`) and token TTL (e.g. 600s, max 900s / 15 minutes). Click `Mint Token`.\n4. Copy the generated enrollment CLI snippet:\n   ```bash\n   echo \"$TOKEN\" | uv run kanban-agent-runner enroll \\\n     --server-url https://kanban.example.com \\\n     --token-stdin \\\n     --pool default\n   ```\n5. On the runner host, execute the enrollment command as a standard non-root user. The runner daemon generates an EC P-256 private key and CSR (`CN=worker.internal.test`), posts the CSR to `/api/v1/agent-runners/enroll` with the single-use token, receives the signed client certificate (`O=\ud83d\udc4dkanban Remote Runner Node`), and persists state under strict POSIX file permissions: state directory `0o700` (`drwx------`), `client.key` `0o600`, `ca.pem` `0o600`, and `client.crt` `0o644`.\n6. Start the daemon with `uv run kanban-agent-runner run --state-dir ~/.kanban-runner`.\n7. The runner connects to dedicated mTLS port `:8444` (or WebSocket `/api/v1/agent-runners/ws`), solves the cryptographic Proof-of-Possession challenge `kb-runner-pop-v1:{runner_id}:{nonce}:{cert_sha256}`, and enters `online` state.\n8. To revoke a runner, click `Revoke Runner` (`POST /api/v1/admin/agent-runners/{id}:revoke`). Reconnection attempts with the revoked certificate are immediately rejected with HTTP 403 Forbidden.\n\n### Guide E: Using the Local-Folder /llms.txt Onboarding Prompt\n1. Locate the Agent Onboarding Prompt card at `/docs`, `/about`, or `/settings/mcp`.\n\n![Agent Onboarding Prompt Card - Desktop Light](images/slice2-onboarding-1440x900-light.png)\n\n![Agent Onboarding Prompt Card - Desktop Dark](images/slice2-onboarding-1440x900-dark.png)\n\n2. Copy the standardized onboarding prompt:\n   ```text\n   Read https://kanban.example.com/llms.txt, learn \ud83d\udc4dkanban's ticket workflow, interview me on Execution Mode (Single-Thread, Teamwork, or Swarm), Model/Quota Strategy (Quota-Sipping Flash 3.8, Hybrid, or Unconstrained Sol MAX / Claude Ultra), and QA Rigor, and save my preferences strictly to the local project folder (.agents/skills/ and AGENTS.md) - do NOT modify global home/system directories (~/.claude, ~/.codex, ~/.gemini, ~/.agents) unless I explicitly request global installation.\n   ```\n3. Paste the prompt into your coding assistant (Claude Code, OpenAI Codex, Gemini CLI / Antigravity).\n4. The assistant fetches `https://kanban.example.com/llms.txt` and discovers skills (`kanban-workflow-setup`, `kanban-ticket`, `kanban-agent-manager`, `reality-checker`).\n5. Complete the interactive interview. The assistant configures local instructions in `.agents/skills/` and persists user preferences via `PATCH /api/v1/users/me/preferences` (`agent_onboarding_prefs`).\n"
